The **Hawthorne Effect** is the phenomenon where individuals modify an aspect of their behavior in response to their awareness of being observed.

In the context of your question, this effect explains why people often perform better, report higher levels of compliance, or exhibit more positive behaviors simply because they know they are being watched or measured, rather than because the measurement itself has caused a fundamental change in their underlying nature.

This concept is frequently applied in fields like psychology, sociology, and organizational management to explain discrepancies between observed behavior and actual behavior when subjects are unaware of being studied.
